Organization: Yggdrasil Computing, Inc.
Date: Sun, 1 May 1994 21:22:23 GMT
Approved: linux-announce@tc.cornell.edu (Matt Welsh)
Lines: 49

	As some of you already know, the upcoming Yggdrasil release
includes a fully buildable source tree with logging versions of cp
and install, enabling the creation of a database for tracing files
back to their original locations in the source tree.

	Because many files live in the same places on other Linux
systems, this facility should also be useful to people who run other
distributions, but who want to find source code quickly.  So, we are
putting this database on the net along the simple "whence" and
"realpath" commmands for accessing it.

	Whence can be used on any file that was installed from the
source build by cp or install, and uses realpath to resolve
symbolic links.  For executable commands, whence will find the
command in your path.  Here is an example.

	% whence csh
	/bin/csh is actually /usr/bin/tcsh
	/usr/bin/tcsh: linux dynamically linked
	/usr/bin/tcsh installed from /usr/src/usr.bin/tcsh-6.04/tcsh
        %

	The human-readable log that whence uses lives in
/usr/src/install.log.  A companion file, /usr/src/components maps
regions of the source tree to component names used by our mailing
lists and bug tracking system.  Currently, whence does not use this
database, but it would be nice to someday extend this system so that 
when a program gets a segmentation fault, a completed bug report 
form pops up.

