General description.
--------------------
Callback 1.21 is out. This package allows you to set up your dial-in modem in
such a way that users can be called back by it. The advantage is of course
that if your Linux box is at the office, the phone bill is picked up by
whoever pays your office phone bill. But additionally, the callback package is
a security measure: only users who are recognized by the user name and by a
callback phone number can gain access to the Linux box.

What's needed.
--------------
To make callback work, you need a preferably Hayes-compatible modem and
getty_ps. I've heard from several people that 2.0.7b works (an older version)
while newer versions don't. Furthermore, you may need setserial. All this
stuff is needed to get dial-in set up correctly.

As for the callback setup itself, you need callback-1.21.tar.gz. This file is
available at:
	beatrix.icce.rug.nl, directory pub/unix (primary site)
	tsx-11.mit.edu, directory pub/linux/sources/usr.bin/Communication
	    (though I don't know when the folks at tsx will get 'round to
	    copying the /incoming file to that directory)
	sunsite.unc.edu, directory pub/Linux/system/Serial (though also here
	    it may take a while before version 1.21 is there).
The archive contains a file callback.doc with further instructions.	    

What's new.
-----------
In this version I somewhat cleaned up the code and rewrote the docs. If you're
currently using version 1.20 but have the special callback-names "extra" and
"direct" disabled, then you don't really have to upgrade.

The lsm entry.
--------------
